        <article class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-6 order-1 lg:order-2">
            <div class="prose prose-invert prose-zinc max-w-none">
                <p class="drop-cap text-lg md:text-xl leading-[1.8] mb-10 text-zinc-300 font-light">
                    To understand the heart of Tuscany is to understand the language of the soil. Beyond the tourist-laden corridors of Florence and the crowded squares of Siena lies a silent landscape of rolling ochre hills and cypress-lined avenues that whisper stories of centuries-old harvests. It is here, in the secluded corners of Val d’Orcia, that the Sangiovese grape doesn't just grow; it survives and transcends.
                </p>
                <p class="text-lg md:text-xl leading-[1.8] mb-10 text-zinc-300 font-light">
                    The morning mist clings to the valley floor like a silken shroud, protecting the vines from the early chill. As the sun ascends, it reveals a mosaic of galestro and alberese limestone—the secret foundation of the region's legendary acidity and structure.
                </p>
                <blockquote class="my-20 relative py-12 px-10 border-l border-accent-burgundy bg-accent-burgundy/5 group">
                    <span class="absolute -top-6 -left-4 text-8xl text-accent-burgundy/20 font-black leading-none pointer-events-none">“</span>
                    <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-display uppercase leading-tight text-white mb-8 tracking-tighter">
                        A great wine is not made in the cellar; it is merely preserved there. The true alchemy happens in the quiet struggle between the vine and the stony earth.
                    </p>
                    <cite class="block text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.3em] font-black text-accent-burgundy">— Lorenzo de’ Medici, Vineyard Master</cite>
                </blockquote>
                <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-black mb-8 text-white mt-20 leading-none">The Geometry of Terroir</h2>
                <p class="text-lg md:text-xl leading-[1.8] mb-10 text-zinc-300 font-light">
                    Traditional viticulture in this region relies on a deep respect for the terrain's natural contours. Unlike the rigid grids of industrial vineyards, these plots follow the organic flow of the hills, maximizing solar exposure while ensuring the wind can dance between the leaves to prevent rot.
                </p>
                <div class="my-16">
                    <div class="overflow-hidden">
                        <img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/aida-public/AB6AXuCi4T4jQHXZC5OsLiLQiftQxCyfsIL6rf2x55NQarRFlXXSazKSfBjVjfnscDha-Y2_gmj-3hisQhhAtDMu22UBBWPR39ggToX9iL1JKxCbyBjJvP3GXmHWF5KjQ4HmaC9ozF4EiyT5PkJQ3ifqnqngkt4_MNhh_vZzvutQ-45rl9iIgzfbpeuviRgRZcaXl6cR542cqjhB2X1zLObqeJkIe37T5OKdENEbSstVw5TMiQX2rrOk1FRn_mSam93PUSd_PkbN297I2B0" alt="Close-up of a glass of red wine in a vineyard setting" class="w-full moody-image">
